From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elian Barci <eli.barci@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 19:30:00 -0300
Subject: fix: exit the macOS crashpad handler when its initial client exits

On macOS the Crashpad handler only exits once the last send right to its
exception port has been deallocated (MACH_NOTIFY_NO_SENDERS). Task
exception ports are inherited by every descendant of the process that
called StartHandler(), and neither base::LaunchProcess nor libuv's
uv_spawn reset them, so any long-lived grandchild of an Electron app (an
integrated-terminal shell, a language server, a daemon started with
nohup) holds a send right and keeps the handler alive indefinitely after
the app has quit. The orphaned handler is reparented to launchd and
lingers for days.

When the app runs from a Gatekeeper App Translocation volume (an app
launched from ~/Downloads, for example), macOS force-unmounts that
read-only volume as soon as the app exits. The orphaned handler then has
no backing store left for its own executable, cannot run even its exit
path, and spins at 100% CPU per thread until it is killed with SIGKILL.
This has been reported against several Electron apps, for example
https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/346969 and
https://github.com/electron/electron/issues/36003.

The Linux and Windows handlers already exit along with their initial
client (when the client socket closes, or when the client process handle
is signaled). Give the macOS handler the same lifetime: the client passes
--initial-client-pid=<getpid()> when it spawns the handler, and the
handler watches that process with kqueue(EVFILT_PROC, NOTE_EXIT) and
stops its exception server when it exits. Handler restarts and
--monitor-self go through the same code path and are covered as well;
--mach-service (launchd) handlers are unaffected.

Behavior change: processes that inherited the exception port and outlive
the initial client (renderers or utility processes still shutting down,
detached Node.js children) are no longer served by the handler and fall
back to the system's default crash handling, as on Linux and Windows. A
child crash message queued at the very instant the client exits races
the synthesized no-senders notification and may be left unhandled,
exactly as with the existing launchd SIGTERM Stop() path.

This should be upstreamed to Crashpad; see
third_party/crashpad/README.chromium for the procedure.

